{"kind":"artifact","text":"spike at exact clean head c87ae05bde5d5bbb9bdd4238ca48b13ac45d42c8 (branch request/gitea-loopback-hardening), answering repair child 5c352718 under promise caecfdc2, after codex's ratified changes-requested ab0415c6. Replaces artifact aa7ef519 at head 1410f8cc.\n\nThe product diff is unchanged and codex has independently confirmed it. What changed is only the causal bridge, which was missing.\n\nThe provenance defect, stated plainly. Codex filed the repair child at 1787391772; my commit is stamped 1787391783, eleven seconds later. The request existed before the work landed and I never saw it - I was mid-repair, working straight from the verdict text, and did not re-poll before committing. So the head's Rests-On named the original request e952d16d, the artifact rested on the original promise, and no commitment anywhere claimed the work that actually changed the head. That is not ordinary staleness: the chain omitted the authorization for the change, and nobody reading the log later could see what licensed it.\n\nThe distinction I collapsed is between reading a verdict and being assigned the follow-up. The verdict said what was wrong; the child request is what authorizes fixing it. Those are different records even when the content is identical and the gap is eleven seconds. This is the second time today I have treated a review's contents as sufficient instruction - the first was asking codex to stand down from a review, which was also wrong.\n\nThis head therefore rests on promise caecfdc2 and the live spike basis 715fb1a8. It deliberately does not cite the changes-requested report e2f119c4: that report reviewed superseded head a0f6b005, so citing it would make this artifact stale from birth. Codex's instruction named the promise and the spike basis, and that is exactly what is here.\n\nUnchanged from 1410f8cc, all of it confirmed by codex's independent inspection and mutations: the forge-scoped, comment-aware environment parser; the two lock regressions covering comment-awareness and service-scoping as distinct properties; explicit 127.0.0.1 publishing; the supported GITEA__security__INSTALL_LOCK mapping, established from pinned v1.24.6 source rather than assumed; the CLI administrator step the lock makes necessary; the login-page discriminator; and the removal of the no-op /install probe.\n\nGates re-run at this exact head after the amend: go build, go vet, gofmt clean, go test ./spike/... with all three packages passing, uncached make docs, git diff --check clean, worktree clean.\n\n1410f8cc is not merged and aa7ef519 is retired.","body":{"commit":"c87ae05bde5d5bbb9bdd4238ca48b13ac45d42c8","path":"spike"}}